/F3 5.88 Tf
65.1968 293.3751 Td
(Description:)Tj
ET
BT
/F2 5.88 Tf
65.1968 286.3191 Td
(The park power curve is similar to a WTG power curve, meaning that when a given wind speed appears in front of the park with same speed in the entire wind farm area \(before influence from)Tj
ET
BT
/F2 5.88 Tf
65.1968 279.2631 Td
(the park\), the output from the park can be found in the park power curve. Another way to say this: The park power curve includes wake losses, but do NOT include terrain given variations in the)Tj
ET
BT
/F2 5.88 Tf
65.1968 272.2071 Td
(wind speed over the park area.)Tj
ET
BT
/F2 5.88 Tf
65.1968 265.1510 Td
(Measuring a park power curve is not as simple as measuring a WTG power curve due to the fact that the park power curve depends on the wind direction and that the same wind speed normally)Tj
ET
BT
/F2 5.88 Tf
65.1968 258.0950 Td
(will not appear for the entire park area at the same time \(only in very flat non-complex terrain\). The idea with this version of the park power curve is not to use it for validation based on)Tj
ET
BT
/F2 5.88 Tf
65.1968 251.0390 Td
(measurements. This would require at least 2 measurement masts at two sides of the park, unless only a few direction sectors should be tested, AND non complex terrain \(normally only useable)Tj
ET
BT
/F2 5.88 Tf
65.1968 243.9831 Td
(off shore\). Another park power curve version for complex terrain is available in windPRO.)Tj
ET
BT
/F3 5.88 Tf
65.1968 229.8711 Td
(The park power curve can be used for:)Tj
ET
BT
/F2 5.88 Tf
79.3701 222.8151 Td
(1.)Tj
ET
BT
/F2 5.88 Tf
93.5433 222.8151 Td
(Forecast systems, based on more rough \(approximated\) wind data, the park power curve would be an efficient way to make the connection from wind speed \(and direction\) to power.)Tj
ET
BT
/F2 5.88 Tf
79.3701 215.7591 Td
(2.)Tj
ET
BT
/F2 5.88 Tf
93.5433 215.7591 Td
(Construction of duration curves, telling how often a given power output will appear, the park power curve can be used together with the average wind distribution for the Wind farm)Tj
ET
BT
/F2 5.88 Tf
93.5433 208.7031 Td
(area in hub height. The average wind distribution can eventually be obtained based on the Weibull parameters for each WTG position. These are found at print menu: >Result to file<)Tj
ET
BT
/F2 5.88 Tf
93.5433 201.6470 Td
(in the >Park result< which can be saved to file or copied to clipboard and pasted in Excel.)Tj
ET
BT
/F2 5.88 Tf
79.3701 194.5911 Td
(3.)Tj
ET
BT
/F2 5.88 Tf
93.5433 194.5911 Td
(Calculation of wind energy index based on the PARK production \(see below\).)Tj
ET
BT
/F2 5.88 Tf
79.3701 187.5350 Td
(4.)Tj
ET
BT
/F2 5.88 Tf
93.5433 187.5350 Td
(Estimation of the expected PARK production for an existing wind farm based on wind measurements at minimum 2 measurement masts at two sides of wind farm. The masts must be)Tj
ET
BT
/F2 5.88 Tf
93.5433 180.4791 Td
(used for obtaining the free wind speed. The free wind speed is used in the simulation of expected energy production with the PARK power curve. This procedure will only work suitable)Tj
ET
BT
/F2 5.88 Tf
93.5433 173.4230 Td
(in non complex terrains. For complex terrain another park power curve calculation is available in windPRO \(PPV-model\).)Tj
ET
BT
/F3 5.88 Tf
65.1968 159.3110 Td
(Note:)Tj
ET
BT
/F2 5.88 Tf
65.1968 152.2551 Td
(From the >Result to file< the >Wind Speeds Inside Wind farm< is also available. These can \(e.g. via Excel\) be used for extracting the wake induced reductions in measured wind speed.)Tj
ET
